1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,800
They are the finest rocket engines in the world, built under conditions of absolute secrecy during Russia's race to the moon.

2
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:14,680
And it all began at Russia's vast launch complex, the Cosmodrome.

3
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,760
Next, on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,120
Behind the doors of this warehouse, in a distant part of Russia,

5
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:45,600
lie what for 20 years was a closely guarded secret.

6
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:51,760
A cache of rocket engines designed for the most ambitious space project

7
00:00:51,761 --> 00:00:54,580
ever to be undertaken by the Soviet Union.

8
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:59,860
Today regarded as some of the finest in the world,

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,440
The engines use a unique technology developed by Russia at the height of the space race.

10
00:01:06,441 --> 00:01:13,280
A technology so ambitious that Western rocket scientists thought it was impossible to master.

11
00:01:27,763 --> 00:01:33,660
The Russians were very secretive at that time, and although they said they were doing something,

12
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:40,820
they did not tell us what they were doing, and in fact it took a great deal of intelligence

13
00:01:40,821 --> 00:01:44,660
to really understand what their direction was.

14
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:52,980
The really important things were having good, clear pictures of what they were building.

15
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:04,000
In the 1960s, the CIA spy satellites were keeping regular watch

16
00:02:04,001 --> 00:02:07,000
on the Soviet Union's vast space launch complex,

17
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:10,440
the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan.

18
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:19,920
In March, April 63, we saw at least some initial what would be called stakes in the grounds

19
00:02:19,921 --> 00:02:24,900
for surveyors and stuff like that in the area that was undeveloped on the Baikonur Cosmodrome.

20
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:31,860
The size of the assembly building, the causeway had all the earmarks for a very large launch

21
00:02:31,861 --> 00:02:36,340
vehicle. Indeed, it was like, hello, what's going on here?

22
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:50,660
The satellite images clearly showed the construction of a new causeway leading up to two huge launchpads.

23
00:02:52,780 --> 00:03:04,020
To CIA analysts back in Washington, the launchpads were the first hard evidence that preparations were underway for the assembly of a huge new rocket.

24
00:03:04,021 --> 00:03:10,360
The sheer size of the complex could mean only one thing.

25
00:03:10,361 --> 00:03:16,780
The Soviets were now in a race with the United States to reach the moon.

26
00:03:16,781 --> 00:03:27,260
In the beginning of the 60s, the Russians were far ahead of us.

27
00:03:27,261 --> 00:03:32,020
They had better rocket engines, so with that rocket power, they were able to do lots of

28
00:03:32,021 --> 00:03:35,420
things that we were simply unable to do. It was a wonderful period.

29
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:53,700
First ICBM in August of 57, Sputnik 1 in October of 1957, and then first man in space, first woman

30
00:03:53,701 --> 00:04:00,020
in space, first three men in space, first extravehicular activity, first spacecraft to hit the moon.

31
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:09,460
Russia's achievements in space were the results of a unique approach to rocket design,

32
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:11,940
pioneered by one central figure,

33
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:15,880
chief designer Sergei Pavlovich Kurelyov,

34
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:18,520
head of OKM-1,

35
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:24,600
and Russia's most senior rocket scientist.

36
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:30,820
He was very much the dominant figure in the Russian space program.

37
00:04:30,821 --> 00:04:35,300
There's simply no counterpart, for example, in the U.S. program.

38
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:48,240
He had responsibility not only for the beginning years, the Sputniks, the man program, communication satellites, spy satellites, planetary spacecraft.

39
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:53,240
There's no American who had that kind of responsibility.

40
00:04:53,241 --> 00:05:00,080
You'd have to equate his work with 15 aerospace companies and about four or five NASA centers.

41
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:03,180
Very, very much the dominant figure.

42
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:09,420
His identity was a state secret.

43
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:16,040
As chief designer, Karelyov was the most powerful figure in the Soviet Space Design Bureau,

44
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,940
reporting directly to the Kremlin.

45
00:05:18,941 --> 00:05:29,940
Karelyov was the key figure behind Russia's unique approach to rocket design, a pragmatic,

46
00:05:29,941 --> 00:05:35,940
hardware-based philosophy in which important design questions were only resolved after

47
00:05:35,941 --> 00:05:40,940
full test flights.

48
00:05:40,941 --> 00:05:48,380
In a lot of respects, they were well ahead of the game, but the reason they were well

49
00:05:48,381 --> 00:05:51,280
ahead of the game is because they had actually put all the hardware together

50
00:05:51,281 --> 00:05:55,080
and had applied that technology far better than we had.

51
00:05:57,780 --> 00:06:02,160
Russia's breakthroughs were the results of the Bureau's hands-on approach.

52
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:06,300
The rockets were built,

53
00:06:06,301 --> 00:06:10,300
flown,

54
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:15,100
and then improved.

55
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:22,340
Conquering space was a political priority.

56
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:29,800
From his headquarters at OKB-1 in the suburbs of Moscow,

57
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:33,740
Russia's most senior rocket scientist, Sergei Karelyev,

58
00:06:33,741 --> 00:06:36,160
started work on a manned lunar mission

59
00:06:36,161 --> 00:06:39,920
with his experimental hardware-based design philosophy.

60
00:06:42,740 --> 00:06:47,240
But the challenge of landing a man on the moon was a different kind of problem.

61
00:06:48,380 --> 00:06:55,040
The United States had raised the stakes by declaring it would land a man on the moon by the end of the 1960s.

62
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:58,860
The odds against Russia were mounting.

63
00:07:18,571 --> 00:07:24,100
Karelyev's deputy, Vasily Mishin, had started working on rockets at OKB-1 after World War II.

64
00:07:24,101 --> 00:07:32,180
To escape the Earth's gravity and reach the Moon, a lunar mission would require a huge new rocket.

65
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:41,880
In order not just to get to the Moon, but to land on the Moon,

66
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:45,980
and to bring back an expedition consisting of at least one person,

67
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:50,580
for that you need to put a hundred tons into the Earth's orbit.

68
00:07:50,581 --> 00:07:58,960
And that means you need to have a rocket which has a starting weight in the region of 2,000 tons.

69
00:08:14,683 --> 00:08:20,580
Karelyov quickly realized that they would need new vastly more powerful engines for the new rocket.

70
00:08:20,581 --> 00:08:30,760
But Karelyov did not see eye to eye with his usual manufacturer on the kind of engines the new rocket would need.

71
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:34,820
He had no choice but to find an alternative supplier.

72
00:08:42,940 --> 00:08:47,140
Karelyov was forced to go to an aircraft designer named Kuznetsov in Samar.

73
00:08:47,141 --> 00:08:50,900
This is a fellow who had designed very good aircraft engines.

74
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:53,200
But that was all the experience he had.

75
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:56,180
That was a big risk for Koryov.

76
00:08:59,440 --> 00:09:03,000
Kuznetsov had a formidable reputation as a jet engineer,

77
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:08,420
having designed the engines for the first Soviet long-range strategic bomber.

78
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:13,280
But he had never worked with rocket engines.

79
00:09:13,281 --> 00:09:23,980
Kuznetsov had no experience in building liquid propellant engines

80
00:09:23,981 --> 00:09:26,340
but when he looked at their configuration

81
00:09:26,341 --> 00:09:30,240
he saw that liquid propellant engines, rocket engines

82
00:09:30,241 --> 00:09:33,560
did not pose any more problems than aircraft engines

83
00:09:33,561 --> 00:09:36,360
and realized that this challenge could be solved

84
00:09:36,361 --> 00:09:42,960
Valentin Enisimov had worked under Kuznetsov

85
00:09:42,961 --> 00:09:49,960
on jet engine design, but when the chance came in 1962 to apply his skills to rocket engineering,

86
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:54,280
he seized it.

87
00:09:54,281 --> 00:09:59,500
From the Kuznetsov Design Bureau in Samara, on the banks of the Volga River,

88
00:09:59,501 --> 00:10:06,300
just over 600 miles from Moscow, the team began working out what kind of engines the colossal

89
00:10:06,301 --> 00:10:08,000
new moon rocket would need.

90
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:15,600
The basic design of rocket engines is simple.

91
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:21,140
Oxygen and fuel are pumped separately at very high pressures into the beating heart of the

92
00:10:21,141 --> 00:10:26,580
engine, the combustion chamber, where they are ignited, propelling the rocket forward.

93
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:31,940
The greater the pressure in the combustion chamber, the better the engine performance.

94
00:10:31,941 --> 00:10:41,940
Those very high pressures are built up by pumps driven by a gas turbine fired from the pre-burner,

95
00:10:41,941 --> 00:10:46,940
a mini-combustion chamber, that draws off part of the oxygen and fuel supply.

96
00:10:54,940 --> 00:10:57,940
Faced with the deadline of beating the United States,

97
00:10:57,941 --> 00:11:03,500
States, it became clear to Karelyov that there wouldn't be time to develop the

98
00:11:03,501 --> 00:11:09,500
kind of massive engines needed for the new rocket, christened the N1. Russia

99
00:11:09,501 --> 00:11:14,280
lacked the industrial infrastructure of the United States. There would only be

100
00:11:14,281 --> 00:11:20,880
time to make small but highly efficient engines for the N1 rocket. A total of 30

101
00:11:20,881 --> 00:11:26,440
for the rocket's first stage. In order to lift off you have to have more thrusts

102
00:11:26,441 --> 00:11:29,200
than the gravitational force holding you down.

103
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:32,820
And in our case, we decided to go with five engines

104
00:11:32,821 --> 00:11:35,360
to do that, provide that thrust.

105
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:38,340
And in the case of the Russians, they used 30 engines.

106
00:11:38,341 --> 00:11:42,160
So they used engines that were about a sixth the thrust level

107
00:11:42,161 --> 00:11:43,760
of our Saturn V engines.

108
00:11:44,980 --> 00:11:50,600
And, you know, it's a matter of engineering choice which way you went.

109
00:11:50,601 --> 00:11:59,640
But the choice to have 30 engines was forced on Karelyov by Russia's lack of resources.

110
00:12:00,440 --> 00:12:07,040
With so many engines, the chances of anyone failing would massively increase.

111
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:16,940
To overcome those huge odds and achieve the efficiency and performance Karelyov demanded,

112
00:12:16,941 --> 00:12:24,760
The engine designers would have to solve a problem always considered too difficult and too dangerous.

113
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:34,260
At that time, all American and Russian rocket engines worked using an open cycle,

114
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:40,180
in which the exhaust products from the pre-burner driving the pumps are dumped over the side.

115
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:42,960
It was a simple but wasteful design.

116
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:50,780
For Karelyov, such inefficiency was unacceptable.

117
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:58,380
Russia's engine designers would have no choice but to take an untried and risky step.

118
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:09,900
They would have to close the cycle and find a way of safely channeling the exhaust products from the pre-burner back into the combustion chamber to be re-fired.

119
00:13:09,901 --> 00:13:16,900
A potentially explosive process, but one that could boost the lifting power of the rocket by 25%.

120
00:13:17,640 --> 00:13:26,980
Coast cycle engines require a much better understanding of the combustion process

121
00:13:26,981 --> 00:13:33,340
and a much better control of all of the stages in that combustion process.

122
00:13:39,900 --> 00:13:44,820
As a consequence, you can blow up a lot of engines trying to build one that works.

123
00:13:46,060 --> 00:13:49,740
And I think that the Russians did blow up quite a lot of engines.

124
00:13:55,560 --> 00:14:01,180
Mastering the new closed-cycle technology with their traditional approach would take time.

125
00:14:01,980 --> 00:14:06,240
Time the Russians didn't have if they wanted to beat the U.S. to the moon.

126
00:14:09,900 --> 00:14:15,320
Not everything went badly.

127
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:21,660
The Bureau's experience with jet engines gave it an innovative approach to rocket engine development.

128
00:14:24,360 --> 00:14:33,260
The Kuznetsov team realized that there were strong similarities between the turbopump assemblies and jet engines and rocket motors.

129
00:14:33,261 --> 00:14:43,180
In the construction of both airplane engines and rocket engines, there is a battle to keep

130
00:14:43,181 --> 00:14:45,920
the weight down.

131
00:14:45,921 --> 00:14:50,000
In our design, the booster pumps are inside the main pumps.

132
00:14:50,001 --> 00:14:56,140
This dramatically reduces the external piping, improves reliability and decreases weight.

133
00:14:56,141 --> 00:15:10,141
The compact design of the engine was both original and elegant.

134
00:15:15,180 --> 00:15:19,180
Well, this design was really a radical design for the early 1960s.

135
00:15:19,620 --> 00:15:25,620
Whenever you're designing engines, you want to make it as simple as you can with the minimal amount of parts.

136
00:15:25,621 --> 00:15:31,640
So the Russians have typically chosen the single-shaft type design for the turbo machinery,

137
00:15:31,940 --> 00:15:35,960
which really simplifies and adds to its efficiency and performance.

138
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:45,460
By 1966, the key technical challenges behind closing the cycle were resolved.

139
00:15:46,320 --> 00:15:52,160
The highly efficient engine the chief designer had asked for was finally within sight.

140
00:15:55,620 --> 00:16:02,420
but the project was then struck a devastating blow

141
00:16:02,421 --> 00:16:08,880
during routine surgery in January 1966

142
00:16:08,881 --> 00:16:11,060
Karelyov died

143
00:16:11,061 --> 00:16:14,860
the one figure with the authority and vision

144
00:16:14,861 --> 00:16:18,600
to beat the Americans was gone

145
00:16:18,601 --> 00:16:29,920
As the N1 rocket finally started to make its way out to the launch pad at the Cosmodrome,

146
00:16:29,921 --> 00:16:34,720
the odds were stacking up against Russia in the race for the moon.

147
00:16:36,700 --> 00:16:45,020
On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union successfully launched the world's first artificial satellite, Sputnik 1.

148
00:16:45,021 --> 00:16:52,480
About the size of a basketball, Sputnik 1 took about 98 minutes to orbit the Earth on its elliptical path.

149
00:16:53,080 --> 00:16:56,080
Cosmodrome will return on Modern Marvels.

150
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:08,360
By 1967, the first N-1 moon rocket was assembled at the Cosmodrome,

151
00:17:08,361 --> 00:17:11,680
standing 35 stories high

152
00:17:11,681 --> 00:17:16,100
and weighing the same as 400 double-decker buses

153
00:17:16,101 --> 00:17:19,260
Russia's answer to America's Saturn V

154
00:17:19,261 --> 00:17:21,080
was an awesome sight

155
00:17:21,081 --> 00:17:26,000
I have to say that prior to every launch during those years

156
00:17:26,001 --> 00:17:27,880
we felt a certain trepidation

157
00:17:27,881 --> 00:17:30,880
we were still affected by the large number of accidents

158
00:17:30,881 --> 00:17:32,920
that we had witnessed with our own eyes

159
00:17:32,921 --> 00:17:35,140
pretty much since 1947

160
00:17:35,141 --> 00:17:45,280
In the wake of Karelyov's death, Vasily Mishin was appointed to take over as chief designer.

161
00:17:46,320 --> 00:17:51,980
He was put in charge of the 24-hour shifts working to prepare the rocket for launch.

162
00:17:54,040 --> 00:18:00,580
Russia lacked the huge facilities needed to ground test all 30 N1 engines as a whole.

163
00:18:00,581 --> 00:18:09,260
The first launch would take place with an untried rocket on top of closed cycle engines.

164
00:18:11,580 --> 00:18:17,580
There would be no other way to finalize the design but the way other Russian rockets had been perfected,

165
00:18:18,220 --> 00:18:21,040
through a series of full test flights.

166
00:18:21,041 --> 00:18:35,041
Because of our limited resources and capacity, we had to do the bulk of the development of the rocket stages during test space flights.

167
00:18:37,040 --> 00:18:46,140
We planned 12 unmanned launches, and we only planned manned flights for the 13th and 14th launches.

168
00:18:51,040 --> 00:18:58,040
The first unmanned test of the rocket took place in February 1969.

169
00:19:05,040 --> 00:19:09,040
A minute into the flight, the rocket exploded.

170
00:19:09,041 --> 00:19:12,040
25 miles from the launch site.

171
00:19:12,041 --> 00:19:15,040
Metallic debris had caught inside an engine.

172
00:19:15,041 --> 00:19:29,041
of course i felt bad do you think you would feel good if your engine blew up with a thrust of four

173
00:19:29,200 --> 00:19:39,200
and a half thousand tons you feel very bad controlling a rocket with 30 engines was

174
00:19:39,201 --> 00:19:40,466
proving difficult.

175
00:19:40,467 --> 00:19:45,600
But with another 11 unmanned launches still to go before the first manned

176
00:19:45,601 --> 00:19:52,240
flight, the engine's control system would be improved before the next launch in July 1969.

177
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:59,520
This is the launch now, you see. It's stopped. Now it's going to fall.

178
00:20:01,120 --> 00:20:08,000
Seconds after takeoff, the rocket fell back onto the pad, exploding with the force of a small

179
00:20:08,001 --> 00:20:11,880
nuclear bomb.

180
00:20:11,881 --> 00:20:15,620
We hadn't left the dugout ten kilometers away when it crashed.

181
00:20:15,621 --> 00:20:16,900
That's what saved us.

182
00:20:16,901 --> 00:20:23,220
If we had come out, we would not have survived.

183
00:20:23,221 --> 00:20:29,880
The rocket had been launched before the ambitious closed-cycle engines were perfected.

184
00:20:29,881 --> 00:20:34,660
While the debris was still being scraped from the floor of the Kazakh desert, the race to

185
00:20:34,661 --> 00:20:37,660
the moon came to an end.

186
00:20:37,661 --> 00:20:51,660
On July 20th, 1969, just two weeks after the N1 disaster, the Americans were celebrating the success of the Apollo 11 mission.

187
00:20:52,660 --> 00:20:55,260
But the Russians didn't give up.

188
00:20:55,860 --> 00:21:02,660
True to their practice of evolving design through actual launches, engines and rocket were steadily improved.

189
00:21:02,661 --> 00:21:08,191
improved. To prevent the intake of debris, filters were fitted to the engines.

190
00:21:08,192 --> 00:21:08,900
The launch

191
00:21:08,901 --> 00:21:14,100
pad was rebuilt for more tests in 1971 and 72.

192
00:21:16,900 --> 00:21:18,920
Well, this is the third launch.

193
00:21:23,060 --> 00:21:28,560
I've only seen this on film. I didn't actually see it in real life. Only the traces.

194
00:21:32,660 --> 00:21:39,660
There's the fire.

195
00:21:41,840 --> 00:21:48,840
But Russia's problems weren't over. The fourth rocket also blew up.

196
00:21:55,380 --> 00:22:02,420
The moon race was lost, but by the early 1970s, the Russian system of refining through test

197
00:22:02,421 --> 00:22:07,700
flights was delivering real improvements in rocket technology.

198
00:22:07,701 --> 00:22:12,820
A new engine designated the NK-33 was being finalized,

199
00:22:12,821 --> 00:22:17,720
and the bugs from previous launch failures worked out.

200
00:22:17,721 --> 00:22:21,480
With the new filters and protection against vibrations,

201
00:22:21,481 --> 00:22:24,300
the high-performing closed-cycle engines

202
00:22:24,301 --> 00:22:29,460
the designers had dreamed of were finally ready to fly.

203
00:22:29,461 --> 00:22:32,880
Experience showed that Kuznetsov's engines could be made reliable,

204
00:22:32,881 --> 00:22:38,280
but for that we needed those very four years by which we lagged behind the United States.

205
00:22:43,220 --> 00:22:48,440
But at the very point the NK-33 engines could prove their worth,

206
00:22:49,360 --> 00:22:51,080
the program was cancelled.

207
00:22:52,540 --> 00:22:57,060
The Politburo's patience with the N1 had finally run out.

208
00:22:59,460 --> 00:23:07,840
On April 19, 1971, the Russians used a proton rocket to successfully launch the first space station into orbit.

209
00:23:08,560 --> 00:23:10,780
The space station was called Salute One.

210
00:23:11,380 --> 00:23:12,779
It would take the U.S.

211
00:23:12,780 --> 00:23:16,720
two years to catch up with its own space station named Skylab.

212
00:23:17,280 --> 00:23:19,580
Modern marvels will return in a moment.

213
00:23:20,440 --> 00:23:26,520
By 1974, the political imperative behind Russia's race to the moon was gone.

214
00:23:26,521 --> 00:23:31,520
As a result, the Kremlin ordered the entire N1 program cancelled

215
00:23:31,521 --> 00:23:35,260
and Mishan was dismissed as chief designer.

216
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:39,620
What if I was unhappy?

217
00:23:40,300 --> 00:23:42,020
These things happen, of course.

218
00:23:43,080 --> 00:23:44,200
But there were a lot of reasons.

219
00:23:45,660 --> 00:23:47,120
I can't say that I was fine.

220
00:23:48,360 --> 00:23:49,060
Things were bad.

221
00:23:49,780 --> 00:23:51,000
But I survived.

222
00:23:51,840 --> 00:23:52,780
I'm still here.

223
00:23:56,520 --> 00:24:03,520
Today at Baikonur, the desert is slowly reclaiming the colossal works of the N1 project.

224
00:24:03,521 --> 00:24:12,520
After cancellation of the program, the Soviet leadership ordered that all remaining N1 rockets and their engines be destroyed.

225
00:24:12,521 --> 00:24:18,520
The entire history of the program was suppressed.

226
00:24:18,521 --> 00:24:25,520
At the Cosmodrome, parts of the rockets were even converted into pigsties.

227
00:24:26,520 --> 00:24:36,680
What does a man feel when he was responsible for the creation of a project, for nurturing

228
00:24:36,681 --> 00:24:43,480
it, when this system was created and it worked, and suddenly, at the height of your involvement

229
00:24:43,481 --> 00:24:51,600
in the creative process, you're told to cut it short, to put a stop to it?

230
00:24:51,601 --> 00:24:55,200
There was a disappointment that is difficult to convey now.

231
00:24:55,201 --> 00:24:58,200
It was a surprise.

232
00:25:25,200 --> 00:25:31,660
The description to me was, I think there's something there, go find out.

233
00:25:32,560 --> 00:25:36,180
So off we go to start developing contacts.

234
00:25:36,360 --> 00:25:37,860
Who do you talk to? How do you get there?

235
00:25:38,680 --> 00:25:43,440
The barriers that we were looking at looked to be almost insurmountable at the time,

236
00:25:43,540 --> 00:25:49,040
but we chewed away at it, finally got the right connections and started seeing some of the data.

237
00:25:49,041 --> 00:25:56,240
the engine had unusually high performance specifications

238
00:25:56,241 --> 00:26:06,920
when we went to moscow the word got around that we were there and we went you know several times

239
00:26:06,921 --> 00:26:13,760
what happened is people started coming to our hotel and one of the people that came was a was

240
00:26:13,761 --> 00:26:19,700
gentleman from a test facility in Salda up in Siberia where they had tested the

241
00:26:19,701 --> 00:26:26,540
NK 33 engine and through him we were able to arrange a meeting with with Mr.

242
00:26:26,541 --> 00:26:32,700
Kuznetsov himself an unprecedented invitation followed to visit the Russian

243
00:26:32,701 --> 00:26:38,520
Design Bureau at its secret location in Samara there was something they wanted

244
00:26:38,521 --> 00:26:40,520
the visitors to see.

245
00:26:44,520 --> 00:26:47,520
It was literally a forest of engines.

246
00:26:50,520 --> 00:26:56,520
There were over 60 engines stored in this one area, just side by side.

247
00:26:58,520 --> 00:27:06,520
To see that kind of quantity of this tremendous asset was absolutely incredible.

248
00:27:08,520 --> 00:27:15,281
He was speechless. There's that saying, it took his breath away.

249
00:27:15,282 --> 00:27:23,520
He saw them and his jaw dropped. There was a forest of engines. He was amazed.

250
00:27:23,521 --> 00:27:33,000
In defiance of Kremlin orders, a stockpile of NK-33 engines left over from the Moon effort

251
00:27:33,001 --> 00:27:39,380
had been kept in a warehouse, safe from prying eyes, for almost 20 years.

252
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:51,200
It was not sensible to consign engines that were designed to last and to work

253
00:27:51,201 --> 00:27:54,960
to the scrapyard to be melted down.

254
00:27:55,380 --> 00:28:02,340
On his own initiative, Kuznetsov collected together all the engines that had been made

255
00:28:02,341 --> 00:28:04,060
and preserved them.

256
00:28:06,280 --> 00:28:09,940
The rockets were now worth millions of dollars.

257
00:28:10,800 --> 00:28:15,900
It looked like Kuznetsov's foresight in preserving the engines might pay off.

258
00:28:15,901 --> 00:28:23,220
But the NK-33 never proved itself in flight.

259
00:28:24,240 --> 00:28:31,940
Aerojet's visiting engineers had serious doubts about the performance specifications being claimed for the engine's closed cycle.

260
00:28:33,780 --> 00:28:37,440
Well, engineers never just say this is fantastic.

261
00:28:37,441 --> 00:28:42,520
We're a very, a bit of skeptical type of people.

262
00:28:42,700 --> 00:28:45,060
We have to prove things to ourselves technically.

263
00:28:45,900 --> 00:28:50,060
We wanted to know for ourselves that we could back up the claims.

264
00:28:53,440 --> 00:28:56,200
They were doing things at a level.

265
00:28:56,320 --> 00:29:03,920
They were operating at levels that some in the United States were saying as recently as 15 years ago,

266
00:29:04,100 --> 00:29:05,240
you can't do that.

267
00:29:05,900 --> 00:29:08,680
And yet they had been doing it for over 10 years.

268
00:29:09,200 --> 00:29:12,040
So they had really protected their technology.

269
00:29:12,041 --> 00:29:16,560
And those of us in the industry did not know how they were doing it.

270
00:29:17,960 --> 00:29:23,700
The only way doubts about the engine's reliability and performance could be resolved

271
00:29:23,701 --> 00:29:28,840
was to do a full test firing, the first in 20 years.

272
00:29:30,560 --> 00:29:36,600
An engine was sent from Samara to the test stand at Aerojet's facility in Sacramento, California,

273
00:29:36,601 --> 00:29:41,340
for a proving test set for October 1995.

274
00:29:42,040 --> 00:29:44,840
Ring up the sequence computer and load sequence.

275
00:29:47,340 --> 00:29:51,540
Well, it is hard to overstate the anxiety that we felt before the test.

276
00:29:52,240 --> 00:29:57,580
My hair had already gone white, and I thought, can it get any whiter with all that worry?

277
00:30:01,780 --> 00:30:05,200
There was worry, stress, every kind of stress.

278
00:30:05,720 --> 00:30:08,880
Nervous, physical, moral, psychological.

279
00:30:09,360 --> 00:30:11,340
Notify when pressure reaches 3,000.

280
00:30:12,040 --> 00:30:14,180
Pressure's up. Pressure's 3,000.

281
00:30:16,680 --> 00:30:22,980
Standby for test. NKP2-Q01-1A-001 on the countdown.

282
00:30:24,120 --> 00:30:26,980
Siren on. Off at the second bell.

283
00:30:29,760 --> 00:30:30,780
Press the camera, Iris.

284
00:30:35,100 --> 00:30:40,520
Countdown. 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.

285
00:30:41,140 --> 00:30:41,840
Fire.

286
00:30:42,040 --> 00:30:52,720
And you're wondering, oh, this is very, very powerful.

287
00:30:53,220 --> 00:30:54,580
Is it going to hold together?

288
00:30:54,740 --> 00:30:56,160
Is our test stand going to work?

289
00:31:01,040 --> 00:31:06,560
The test conductor is calling out certain critical things,

290
00:31:06,660 --> 00:31:09,800
and in your mind you're saying, yes, that's right, that's right.

291
00:31:09,900 --> 00:31:10,980
It's good, it's good.

292
00:31:12,040 --> 00:31:14,400
The engine hit each of its specifications.

293
00:31:15,840 --> 00:31:19,380
Russia's alternative engineering approach had delivered a technology that,

294
00:31:19,381 --> 00:31:24,380
although 20 years old, America had still not equaled.

295
00:31:30,420 --> 00:31:36,620
We tested it at 10 a.m. That was about midnight here.

296
00:31:37,480 --> 00:31:41,000
Everybody was waiting for us to call and let them know how the test had gone.

297
00:31:41,001 --> 00:31:44,360
And we said, it's okay. It worked.

298
00:31:49,320 --> 00:31:57,080
Aerojet now had all the proof it needed to go out and sell the engines for use on the latest generation of satellite launchers.

299
00:31:57,081 --> 00:32:07,720
One man who knows a thing or two about the rival merits of both Russian and American space technology

300
00:32:07,721 --> 00:32:13,860
is George Muller, an engineer once at the helm of America's race to the moon.

301
00:32:14,120 --> 00:32:22,140
I first came across the NK-33 when we began the development of the K-1, our reusable launch vehicle.

302
00:32:22,141 --> 00:32:29,300
We were looking for high-performance engines, and we knew about the Russian engines in general,

303
00:32:29,540 --> 00:32:34,880
but it wasn't until then that we really began to understand how efficient they really were.

304
00:32:36,500 --> 00:32:44,480
The efficiency of these engines is very good, better than anything else available in the world today.

305
00:32:45,320 --> 00:32:48,460
Start flight pressurization. Roger, going to flight pressures.

306
00:32:48,461 --> 00:32:57,580
The NK-33's closed-cycle technology has now been taken one step further in a new Russian engine.

307
00:32:58,340 --> 00:33:04,660
That engine is about to make history by being the first to fly on an American rocket.

308
00:33:06,260 --> 00:33:14,620
That rocket started life as an intercontinental ballistic missile pointed at Russia.

309
00:33:18,460 --> 00:33:27,760
November 3rd, 1957, marks the day when a Siberian mixed husky named Laika was sent into outer space on board the Russian satellite Sputnik 2.

310
00:33:28,560 --> 00:33:33,940
Affectionately called Mutnik by Americans, Laika was the first animal to orbit the Earth.

311
00:33:34,600 --> 00:33:37,760
Cosmodrome will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

312
00:33:37,761 --> 00:33:47,380
Rocket scientist John Karras of American space giant Lockheed Martin is on his way out to the launch pad at Cape Canaveral, Florida,

313
00:33:47,800 --> 00:33:55,380
just a few days before the first flight of the latest Atlas rocket, powered by a Russian RD-180 engine.

314
00:33:56,060 --> 00:34:05,280
Twice the size of the NK-33, the new RD-180 takes Russian closed-cycle technology to new levels of performance,

315
00:34:05,281 --> 00:34:10,040
offering thrust levels almost five times those of a jumbo jet.

316
00:34:11,040 --> 00:34:16,160
If the launch goes as planned, the RD-180 will enter the history books

317
00:34:16,161 --> 00:34:20,220
as the first Russian booster to power an American rocket

318
00:34:20,221 --> 00:34:23,900
and its communication satellite payload into orbit.

319
00:34:24,220 --> 00:34:26,760
We're working a couple small items this morning, really one item.

320
00:34:26,980 --> 00:34:31,060
So we're doing final checks and we're closing things out.

321
00:34:31,061 --> 00:34:33,840
In about an hour from now, we'll be actually buttoned up for launch

322
00:34:33,841 --> 00:34:37,960
and we really won't do anything the rest of today, Friday, Saturday or Sunday

323
00:34:37,961 --> 00:34:40,060
and we'll come in and power up on Monday and go for launch.

324
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:55,000
The RD-180 is a direct descendant of the huge closed cycle engine

325
00:34:55,001 --> 00:34:58,500
built in the 70s for the aborted Russian space shuttle program.

326
00:34:58,501 --> 00:35:07,060
It's been tailor-made for Lockheed Martin by the NPO Energomash Design Bureau, today

327
00:35:07,061 --> 00:35:13,700
one of the leading manufacturers of rocket engines in the world.

328
00:35:13,701 --> 00:35:19,640
Though Energomash has successfully powered over 2,000 rockets with 11,000 engines, this

329
00:35:19,641 --> 00:35:23,100
will be the Design Bureau's first launch from Cape Canaveral.

330
00:35:23,101 --> 00:35:26,180
and he always wants to make sure that he is taken care of what he is.

331
00:35:28,400 --> 00:35:32,060
Where the old Atlas rocket used to be driven by five engines,

332
00:35:32,580 --> 00:35:37,500
the RD-180 is so powerful that the new Atlas III will use just one

333
00:35:37,501 --> 00:35:41,020
and still offer 10% higher performance.

334
00:35:42,300 --> 00:35:46,700
This remarkable marriage of a Russian engine and American rocket

335
00:35:46,701 --> 00:35:50,920
is the result of a long and sometimes difficult collaboration

336
00:35:50,921 --> 00:35:53,560
between two Cold War rivals.

337
00:35:56,940 --> 00:35:59,700
Bob Ford was one of the first people

338
00:35:59,701 --> 00:36:04,160
to assess an Ergomash's technology in the early 1990s.

339
00:36:05,780 --> 00:36:10,560
Our initial impressions was that we were being fed a line

340
00:36:10,561 --> 00:36:12,440
of something as a come on,

341
00:36:12,620 --> 00:36:14,540
and we couldn't understand how they could be getting

342
00:36:14,541 --> 00:36:16,820
the type of parameters they were talking about.

343
00:36:17,360 --> 00:36:20,540
They were talking about chamber pressure inside the engine

344
00:36:20,541 --> 00:36:23,360
that were higher than anything that we had in the United States.

345
00:36:24,100 --> 00:36:26,460
The chamber pressures of the Russian engine

346
00:36:26,461 --> 00:36:29,720
were higher than those of America's boosters on the space shuttle.

347
00:36:30,220 --> 00:36:34,720
They outclassed standard rocket motors by a factor of four to one.

348
00:36:35,580 --> 00:36:38,420
We thought maybe we misunderstood each other.

349
00:36:39,100 --> 00:36:43,100
We were being told things that we couldn't validate against our own experience,

350
00:36:43,340 --> 00:36:47,100
and we had to go off and check, because it just didn't...

351
00:36:47,101 --> 00:36:48,940
It wasn't the same technology we were used to.

352
00:36:48,941 --> 00:36:52,720
It was a paradigm shift in what we were expecting.

353
00:36:53,220 --> 00:37:00,000
The engine design itself was a departure from what the Americans would do for an engine design.

354
00:37:00,440 --> 00:37:06,080
All the oxygen inside the engine actually flows through the pre-burner, what actually drives the turbine.

355
00:37:07,320 --> 00:37:15,600
The oxygen-rich closed cycle would mean far higher combustion chamber pressures, but it was not without risks.

356
00:37:15,601 --> 00:37:21,380
The hot oxygen gas coming from the pre-burner is so flammable

357
00:37:21,381 --> 00:37:25,500
that even the engine itself can catch fire and explode

358
00:37:25,501 --> 00:37:32,180
If you had an ignition source, you could, and we've seen it on some engines

359
00:37:32,181 --> 00:37:33,940
actually just burn the engine up

360
00:37:33,941 --> 00:37:36,440
The aftermath is something to see

361
00:37:36,441 --> 00:37:40,740
It looks like you've seen large castings that probably are three inches thick

362
00:37:40,741 --> 00:37:43,560
literally melt like candle wax

363
00:37:43,561 --> 00:37:51,560
From its factory on the outskirts of Moscow,

364
00:37:52,280 --> 00:37:58,040
Energarmash has since World War II produced over 100 engine designs,

365
00:37:58,400 --> 00:38:00,840
each one an improvement on the last.

366
00:38:02,280 --> 00:38:04,080
Through incremental development,

367
00:38:04,640 --> 00:38:07,220
it succeeded in mastering a combustion cycle

368
00:38:07,221 --> 00:38:11,320
previously rejected as too dangerous by U.S. rocket scientists.

369
00:38:13,560 --> 00:38:17,380
It's not only the Americans who didn't manage to do that,

370
00:38:17,780 --> 00:38:21,760
but also the Europeans, the Japanese, and the Chinese.

371
00:38:22,380 --> 00:38:27,120
To do this, we had to approach how the fuel is burned in the pre-burner very carefully

372
00:38:27,121 --> 00:38:29,240
so that the temperature field is uniform

373
00:38:29,241 --> 00:38:33,860
and to choose the right kind of materials and production techniques.

374
00:38:37,160 --> 00:38:40,700
Energomash found a way of using the closed cycle

375
00:38:40,701 --> 00:38:43,160
to take chamber pressures to new limits.

376
00:38:43,560 --> 00:38:47,060
while protecting the inside of the motor from the greater risk of fire.

377
00:38:48,360 --> 00:38:53,600
A whole new class of high-temperature-resistant stainless steels, unseen in the West,

378
00:38:54,060 --> 00:38:57,680
was invented to cope with the hazardous, oxygen-rich environment.

379
00:38:59,020 --> 00:39:02,960
That technology dispelled Lockheed Martin's skepticism.

380
00:39:04,980 --> 00:39:06,900
It was very easy to persuade them.

381
00:39:07,780 --> 00:39:11,100
We said, here's the engine, here it is in the production unit,

382
00:39:11,540 --> 00:39:13,000
here it is at the test center,

383
00:39:13,560 --> 00:39:15,560
and now we're going to test it.

384
00:39:15,561 --> 00:39:17,560
Look at the data.

385
00:39:17,561 --> 00:39:21,560
What better way to present it than with a concrete test

386
00:39:21,561 --> 00:39:23,560
of a specific iron engine

387
00:39:23,561 --> 00:39:26,560
to be persuaded that it works and works reliably.

388
00:39:26,561 --> 00:39:29,560
Attention on complex 36A.

389
00:39:29,561 --> 00:39:33,560
There'll be loud venting on the east side of APAD RAM.

390
00:39:33,561 --> 00:39:38,560
At Cape Canaveral, the ground controllers have started their final checks.

391
00:39:38,561 --> 00:39:51,680
Today the RD-180 engine offers performance and reliability beyond anything achieved in the United States with its billions of dollars of investment in space technology.

392
00:39:53,120 --> 00:40:02,520
Exposure to Russian design philosophy has forced American technology giant Lockheed Martin to question its own working methods.

393
00:40:02,521 --> 00:40:05,200
Clear back except for the green team.

394
00:40:05,201 --> 00:40:09,220
The Russians design engineers design the engine.

395
00:40:09,221 --> 00:40:10,720
They have all the drawings.

396
00:40:10,880 --> 00:40:17,140
They hand the drawings across to the manufacturing engineers that are on the floor that are actually going to build this thing.

397
00:40:17,260 --> 00:40:19,840
And those folks actually take over the design at that time.

398
00:40:19,940 --> 00:40:25,760
Whereas in the United States, the design engineer would design something, and he'll hand it across to the factory floor.

399
00:40:25,820 --> 00:40:29,280
And the factory floor will say, I've got to meet this drawing.

400
00:40:30,180 --> 00:40:34,120
And what happens is cost of articles go up, scrap rate goes up.

401
00:40:34,121 --> 00:40:37,300
requirements for tighter and tighter machines go up

402
00:40:37,301 --> 00:40:41,620
and we turn the factory into more of a technology lab.

403
00:40:43,400 --> 00:40:45,140
Back at Kuznetsov,

404
00:40:45,400 --> 00:40:48,360
the team that designed and built the NK-33

405
00:40:48,361 --> 00:40:50,120
is still together.

406
00:40:51,600 --> 00:40:53,600
Though now in his 70s,

407
00:40:53,960 --> 00:40:57,820
Valentin Anisimov remains chief designer of engines.

408
00:40:58,500 --> 00:41:01,660
He and the team are ready to resume production

409
00:41:01,661 --> 00:41:03,660
of the NK-33.

410
00:41:04,120 --> 00:41:08,880
We've been waiting for this for a long time, for 20 years.

411
00:41:09,660 --> 00:41:11,880
All the decisions on that have already been taken.

412
00:41:12,840 --> 00:41:16,180
Production of these engines will be restored in Samara.

413
00:41:18,400 --> 00:41:21,400
Atlas mission controllers take their places for the countdown.

414
00:41:22,000 --> 00:41:29,080
With the launch of this new rocket, Russian rocket engine technology could finally come in from the cold.

415
00:41:29,081 --> 00:41:35,040
temperatures in the mid to upper 70s, and right now holding at 60% chance of no-go at the beginning of the window,

416
00:41:35,720 --> 00:41:39,440
however improving conditions as we approach the second half of the window.

417
00:41:41,800 --> 00:41:46,820
Energomash's team will be able to monitor the engine's performance from their own control room

418
00:41:46,821 --> 00:41:49,360
for the duration of the three-minute burn.

419
00:41:49,600 --> 00:41:51,700
Spacecraft on internal power. Roger.

420
00:41:52,020 --> 00:41:55,160
Mission report to resume countdown. Atlas systems propulsion. Go.

421
00:41:55,280 --> 00:41:56,900
Hydraulics. Go. Dramatics. Go.

422
00:41:56,901 --> 00:42:10,300
10 years after the end of the Cold War, old-fashioned hands-on Russian engineering

423
00:42:10,301 --> 00:42:15,200
is about to teach American high technology an uncomfortable lesson.

424
00:42:15,201 --> 00:42:19,805
This is Atlas Mission Control.

425
00:42:19,806 --> 00:42:31,011
That's U-10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, and the RD-180 engine roars to life.

426
00:42:31,012 --> 00:42:33,160
And a liftoff!

427
00:42:45,200 --> 00:42:59,200
The idea that the technology in the United States was not equal or superior to the Russians

428
00:43:00,700 --> 00:43:03,140
was a very hard pill for a lot of people to swallow.

429
00:43:04,040 --> 00:43:08,860
It was very difficult to tell somebody in the government that all this money that they

430
00:43:08,861 --> 00:43:13,740
put into it really, there was still a technology edge over in Russia over what we got here.

431
00:43:15,200 --> 00:43:27,940
And everything continues to look normal on the engine, staying steady at 87% thrust.

432
00:43:33,940 --> 00:43:35,620
Engine framers continue to look good.

